   "name": "Uses of active and inactive air"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Oxygen is the active part of air: it takes part in burning, rusting and breathing. Nitrogen is the inactive part: it does not react easily.\nUses of oxygen:\n• breathing (respiration) of plants and animals;\n• in hospitals for patients with breathing problems;\n• divers and mountain climbers carry it;\n• burning fuels; welding and cutting metals (oxy-acetylene flame).\nUses of nitrogen:\n• to make ammonia and fertilisers;\n• to keep food fresh in packets (it does not react);\n• liquid nitrogen for freezing.\nArgon fills light bulbs; carbon dioxide is used by plants in photosynthesis and in fire extinguishers.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "u": "a crisp packet is filled with nitrogen"
  },
  "problem": "Why is {u}?",
  "steps": [
   "Nitrogen is inactive: it does not react with the food.",
   "Oxygen would make the oil in the crisps go bad."
  ],
  "answer": "Nitrogen keeps the food fresh because it does not react.",
  "checks": []
 },
